- [x] 1. Measure Colocalization (2026-10-24, `algos/measure/colocalization.rs`; named so beside the object step "Colocalization"): Pearson, slope, Manders M1/M2 (threshold % of max, default 15), overlap, K1/K2, RWC 1/2, Manders M1/M2 with Costes thresholds (Faster = bisection by default, Fast, Accurate, Off) - per image and, with "Per object of", per object (no slope per object, as CellProfiler). CellProfiler's arithmetic exactly incl. its quirks (`>` per image, `>=` per object; RWC ranks over the image / all objects; per-object Costes uses the image's thresholds; Python float `//` in Costes) - fixture `crates/core/tests/colocalization.json` (6 cases, 103 objects, every Costes method). Citations checked on Crossref. 2048², 2601 objects: 35 ms (no Costes) / 211 ms (Faster) / 749 ms (Fast), ~90 MB.
  - [x] Look at the step and its values in the real app. - checked in the real app (2026-10-26)
  - [x] The old "Pearson Correlation" step is removed (2026-10-24) - Measure Colocalization measures Pearson (key `<id>/pearson`). Projects with a Pearson step won't load (unreleased: the step existed 2026-10-22..24).
  - [ ] Inside a class region (`MeasurementRegion::Class`) - CellProfiler doesn't have it (only per object); later if wanted.
- [x] 4. Measure Object Neighbors (2026-10-24, `algos/measure/object_neighbors.rs`): neighbours (expand until adjacent / adjacent / within a distance), percent touching, first/second closest distance (centre to centre, `related_object_id` = that object), angle between them; neighbours of the same or another class. CellProfiler's MeasureObjectNeighbors exactly (fixture `crates/core/tests/object_neighbors.json`, 7 cases, 229 objects incl. the closest objects) - except: no closest object → no value (CellProfiler writes 0). Per object on patches (whole slides fine); "expand until adjacent" grows the whole image (≤ 128 MP). 8192², 42k objects: adjacent 0.27 s, within 35 px 5.5 s, expand 3.1 s / 925 MB.
  - [x] Measure Distance To Parent (`algos/measure/distance_to_parent.rs`): child centre → parent centre and → parent edge (CellProfiler RelateObjects' distances, fixture `crates/core/tests/distance_to_parent.json`), `related_object_id` = the parent; a separate measure step (Relate is an object step).
  - [x] Results (2026-10-24): `Column::MeasurementObject` ("‹step› · ‹name› · object": the id the value names) for measurements declared `naming_objects()` (`measurement_info.related_objects`); a click on it opens that object (`RelatedTo::RelatedOf`), a click on a parent's id too (`RelatedTo::ParentOf`, was not clickable).
  - [x] Look at both steps in the real app. - checked in the real app (2026-10-26)
- [x] 6. Measure Texture (2026-10-24, `algos/measure/texture.rs`): Haralick's 13 features (CellProfiler's MeasureTexture → mahotas.features.haralick) at a scale (default 3) and gray levels (default 256), over the image and, with "Per object of", per object (pixels outside the object and at 0 left out, as CellProfiler). Value for value (1e-9) - fixture `crates/core/tests/texture.json`, 37 objects × 3 scale/level cases, image split over 2 tiles; incl. mahotas' difference variance (variance of the n values of P(|i-j|)). 8192², 74k objects: 0.82 s.
  - Deviations: "Directions" = mean of the 4 (default; keys `<id>/<feature>`) or each (`<feature>_<0|45|90|135>`, CellProfiler's 4 columns); an object with no pixel pair in a direction gets no values (CellProfiler writes 0). Values in 8 bits over the image's bit depth (CellProfiler: over the file type's range).
  - [x] Look at the step and its values in the real app. - checked in the real app (2026-10-26)
  - [ ] Several scales in one step (CellProfiler allows a list); today one step per scale.
- [x] Reproducible results + whole-slide speed (2026-10-24, from the performance-check project): ties in Coloc (best partner), Voronoi (equidistant pixels) and Relate went by `ObjectId` (UUID v7, random within a ms) - now by position (`Object::position_cmp`, first pixel in reading order = CellProfiler's label order); runs give identical objects (before: 9-15 / 1,657 rows differed). Whole slide 131 → 76 s, 9.2 → 8 GB: Tile Merge and Voronoi measure tile by tile (`Object::measure_intensities_of`); the median/quartile histogram now works on raw gray values. Debug-level timings per step/phase. BENCHMARK.md.
  - [x] The other whole-image steps that measure one object at a time (Object Math, Transform Objects, Coloc overlap regions, Propagation, Load Annotated Objects): `measure_intensities_of` too. - done: Object Math, Transform Objects, Coloc, Propagation, Load Annotated Objects use it (checked 2026-10-26)
  - [x] Whole slide's tile phase (2026-10-24): reading is 35-41 of ~55 s per tile - JPEG 2000 (5 z × 5 channels, ~8 ms per 512² tile in the image reader; its part, not EVAnalyzer's). Median Subtract 174-183 → 33 s CPU (sliding histogram, bit-identical); whole slide 76 → 68 s.
  - [ ] Rolling Ball is now the biggest step (69 s CPU, 3.3 s per tile).
  - [x] A test that runs a project twice and with 1 / 4 / 8 threads and expects identical objects (`a_run_gives_the_same_objects_every_time_and_with_any_number_of_threads`: Voronoi, Coloc and Relate ties; fails without the tie-break fixes).
  - [ ] performance-check: its "Commit" column is that repo's commit, not EVAnalyzer's.
- [x] 3. Shape (2026-10-24), checked against CellProfiler's MeasureObjectSizeShape (fixture `crates/core/tests/object_shape.json`, 43 irregular objects incl. rings, bars, single pixels):
  - [x] Feret was wrong: max = the bounding box's diagonal, min = the ellipse's minor axis. Now calipers over the pixel centres (rotating calipers on the hull) = CellProfiler's Min/MaxFeretDiameter exactly. `objects.feret_diameter_px` / `min_feret_px` (+ nm) and Classify Objects' Feret filter use it - **objects passing a Feret filter can change in existing pipelines** (tell the testers).
  - [x] `Column::MaxFeret/MinFeret/MajorAxis/MinorAxis/EquivalentDiameter/Extent`: Max/Min Feret, Major/Minor axis (already = CellProfiler's), and computed in SQL (no column): Extent (area / box area), Equivalent diameter (√(4A/π)).
  - [x] New step "Measure Object Shape" (`algos/measure/object_shape.rs`): convex area (scikit-image's convex hull image), solidity as CellProfiler (area / convex area), Euler number (8-connected), max/mean/median radius (exact distance transform, integer Felzenszwalb-Huttenlocher) - a step, not fixed columns: ~10 µs per 24 px object (writing 3.8M objects took 180 s instead of 54 s with them in every export).
  - [x] Decided 2026-10-24: keep ImageJ's perimeter / circularity / roundness (traced perimeter, documented) and EVA's solidity (hull over pixel corners). They differ from CellProfiler's: FormFactor uses scikit-image's perimeter, which counts a ring's hole edge (ring 0.53 vs EVA 0.83; a single pixel: EVA circularity 1.57); CellProfiler's solidity = area / convex area in pixels (in Measure Object Shape). Mention in the docs/help so CellProfiler users aren't surprised.
  - [x] Tell the testers: Feret changed (see above) - Feret filters in Classify Objects can let other objects through. - in the testers' release notes (2026-10-26)
  - [ ] Orientation (CellProfiler: degrees, scikit-image's convention) - EVA's `major_axis_angle` is radians in another convention; not offered as a column.
  - [ ] Compactness: EVA P²/A vs CellProfiler P²/(4πA) with its perimeter - not offered.
  - [ ] Zernike moments (later).

## Relate objects and Coloc (decided 2026-10-23, design canvas "Object relations in the results", VISION.md §7.0)

Two meanings, two commands, two link columns (like CellProfiler's RelateObjects / MaskObjects split):
- Coloc = "touches / at the same place": equal objects, no direction, many ↔ many. Marks objects (a class) and stores all partners (`coloc_partner_ids`).
- Relate = "belongs to": hierarchy, at most one parent per class (`parent_ids`). Every count/mean per parent comes from Relate.
Rule for users: "Is X part of Y? / per cell" → Relate. "Do X and Y touch? / with which?" → Coloc. Both: Coloc marks (e.g. "ch1 with ch2"), Relate links that class to cells. No backwards compatibility (alpha).

Data model:
- [x] `Object.parent_id` + `children` → `parents: IndexMap<ObjectClass, ObjectId>` (at most one parent per class; key = the parent class set in Relate). Only Relate writes it (no public setter elsewhere); `end_scope` keeps clearing freed links.
- [x] Voronoi, Propagation, ObjectMath, `Object::overlaps` (coloc overlap objects), TransformObjects: stop writing `parent_id` (nothing reads it today).
- [x] DuckDB `objects.parent_id VARCHAR` / `children VARCHAR` → `parent_ids MAP(INTEGER, UUID)`; CSV file export the same. Children are `GROUP BY parent_ids[class]`, no column.
- [x] `coloc_partner_ids MAP(INTEGER, UUID[])` stays, meaning "touches": all partners per class, on both sides.

Relate objects (new command, category Object, `WholeImage`, after TileMerge; its own code - shares only `BboxGrid` and `Object::overlaps` with Coloc):
- [x] Settings: parent classes (one or more - a child gets one parent per class, e.g. Cell and Region), child classes, rule "centre inside" (default) / "largest overlap", children without a parent: keep / remove.
- [x] Owns `parents[parent class]` for every object of its child classes: sets or clears it.
- [x] Checked against CellProfiler RelateObjects on the same labels: "largest overlap" gives every child the same parent as CellProfiler's own `relate_labels` (fixture `crates/core/tests/relate_objects.json`). Benchmark in BENCHMARK.md.
- [ ] Preview (board 5): every link while a Relate step is open (children in their parent's colour, lines), children without a parent as a dashed orange ring, "312 of 340 linked · 28 without a parent" in the settings. Today: the selected object's family (above). Needs the selected step (PipelinesPanelState.selected-step-id, UI only) passed to the viewer (decided 2026-10-23: later).
- [ ] Ready-made modules: "Cell from nucleus" (Propagation/Voronoi → Relate → ObjectMath), "Colocalizing spots per cell" (Coloc marks → Relate to cells).
- [x] Distances (to the parent's centre/edge) as object measurements in the `measurements` table - Measure Object Distance To Parent (2026-10-24).
- [ ] Later: "at least X % overlap" rule.

Coloc (one job: mark by overlap + record who touches whom):
- [x] Settings (2026-10-23, simplified after trying it): "Colocalize" (the class list, as before - every class's objects checked, partners recorded on all sides), "But not", "By at least", "Partners", "Overlap regions as", and optional "Mark objects of" + "As" (the colocalizing objects of that class - or of every class when empty - get a class). An earlier one-sided version ("Mark objects of" required) silently gave nothing when left empty.
- [x] Coloc "All combinations" (off by default): every overlapping pair is recorded, also of objects not overlapping every class - one step with A, B, C shows A∩B, A∩C, B∩C and A∩B∩C in the partner columns; "As" and the overlap regions still need every class. Replaces chaining 2-way and 3-way steps (which overwrote each other's partner lists) - 2026-10-23.
- [x] Partners: all by default, stored on both sides (today's "Allow multi"); option "only the best-overlapping partner" (today's 1:1). The hidden 1:1 default that capped cells at 1 spot goes.
- [x] Multiplicity "Multi coloc only for selected" (`MultiFor`): stays in Advanced for now, to be removed later (Relate covers it).
- [x] Help text: "marked" (A overlaps B and C, each on its own) is not "all three share an area" (the overlap region of A∩B∩C, only where it exists). - done 2026-10-26: "Colocalize", "Overlap regions as" and "As" say it (`coloc_objects.rs`)
- [x] ClassifyObjects "Intersecting With" (`overlapping_with`): stays in Advanced for now, to be removed later (Coloc's marking covers it; "bright and overlapping" = Coloc marks, then Classify on that class).

Results (aggregates computed by the queries, nothing stored):
- [x] List "Children" group (board 1): child classes (with the Relate step that linked them) × the Aggregate picker (Count, Mean, Median, Sum, Min, Max) × the ticked columns; columns grouped under the class; saved in the view (`ViewSettings`). Same values in Plate, Charts, Export, CLI.
- [x] Drill-down (boards 3, U5): a click on a children's count (blue) opens those children, a bar "Spot of Cell …" with ← Back above the list - 2026-10-23.
  - [x] The same for a partner count (Coloc): "Spot ch2 touching …" - 2026-10-23.
- [x] List "Linked objects" group (boards U1, U12): Parent (Relate) - its id (click → its row) and chosen columns; Partners (Coloc) - their ids (click → their rows) and their count per class ("touches N"). No means of partner values (that is Relate's job). Filter All / With a link / Without (U6).
- [x] Row type "A row per · Objects | Images | Overlapping pairs" (board U12): one row per partner pair from `coloc_partner_ids`, both objects' columns side by side; offered when the results have partners. Replaces the "Coloc details" switch (list + export dialog; `ListFilter::with_coloc_details`, `ViewSettings::coloc_details`, `build_coloc_detail_rows` become the row type).
- [x] Column "Coloc with ‹class›" → "Partners ‹class› · Count" (per object; aggregates like any column). Per image "how many overlap" = the count of the marking class. "% overlapping" later with math between columns.
- [x] Split view (board 4): selected object keeps the crosshair, its children light blue, its parent yellow, the rest dimmed; card "Children: Spot · 2", "Parent: Cell" - 2026-10-23.
- [x] Editor object details: "Parent: Cell", "Children: Spot · 7", "Coloc with" → "Touches" - 2026-10-23. In the editor's viewer too: the selected object's children light blue, its parent yellow, lines to them, the rest dimmed.
- [x] Export: the new values are ordinary `Column` variants (child aggregates, parent columns, partner count) with `key()`/`from_key()`, so every export kind, the CLI `--columns`/`--view` and Plate/Charts get them; the export dialog's column picker gets the Children and Linked objects groups; the Objects export follows the row type (pairs replace the "List (Coloc Details)" sheet / `list_coloc_details.csv`).
- [x] CLI: `export --colocalized` and `--split-colocalized` (always failed: "isn't supported by the current results backend") removed - 2026-10-23.
- [x] Tell the alpha testers: "spots per cell" with Coloc 1:1 (the default) counted at most 1 spot per cell - redo it with Relate. - in the testers' release notes (2026-10-26)
- [x] `get_centroid()` is the centre of mass (was the bbox centre, now `get_bbox_center()`); both stored (`centroid_*`, `bbox_center_*`) - 2026-10-23, BENCHMARK.md.
- [ ] Linked objects: the parent's columns are the ticked ones (one picker less than board U1's "Its columns"); a picker of its own if users need other columns.
- [x] Editor viewer: the selected object was drawn magenta-ish, not yellow - `Color::from_argb_u8(0xfc, 0xe9, 0x03, alpha)` had the alpha last (Slint: alpha first) - 2026-10-23.
- [x] Results split view: the image drawer's OBJECTS list picks which classes are drawn on the split image (session only, not saved in the view); "Overlapping pairs" → "Coloc pairs", "Partners X · Count" → "Coloc with X" - 2026-10-23.
- [x] Results split view: the drawer shows the editor's Classes panel (the analysis' classes, their objects on the image; eye = drawn, click = on top) instead of an OBJECTS list; objects drawn and picked by the editor's code (`editor/object_render.rs`: compositor, layers selected object > selected class > list order > unclassified, selection/family colors, lines to relatives, click picks the top one) - no ellipses any more, results without masks show filled boxes; the objects' opacity slider sets their fill; a click found the wrong object with a class hidden (overlay index ≠ object index) - fixed - 2026-10-23.

## Whole image preview and object masks (2026-10-23)

- [x] Whole image preview: top bar button / Shift+F5 / command palette - the visible tiles first, then the rest (4096 px tiles, `PreviewRequest::whole_image`, core `process_all_tiles`). Class counts and the object list are then the whole image's; the pill stays with "Whole image: N objects". A setting change cancels a running one and marks a finished one outdated; another image closes it. Refused above 16 full tiles (a whole slide: run the analysis).
- [x] Whole image preview without a tile limit (2026-10-25): the whole image means all of it, also a whole slide - the 16-tile refusal is gone (the viewport's own limit stays).
- [ ] Whole image preview on a whole slide: a warning with the estimated time first, if users ask for it (it may take long and keep many objects in RAM).
- [x] Object masks in the results (`objects.mask BLOB`, run lengths, `storage/mask_codec.rs`), switch Settings › Objects › "Keep object outlines in the results" (`resultsStorage.storeObjectMasks`, on by default). Results' split view draws them (ellipses without). Cost in BENCHMARK.md.
- [ ] Editor: an object's mask from the results (e.g. compare a run with the preview).

## 3D support (planned 2026-10-25)

Goal: volumes (z-stacks analysed as 3D) without breaking the project schema, the results DB or the remote protocol once implementation starts. scopeio needs nothing new: it reads one (z, t) plane per `RegionRequest`, `read_region_into` can fill a preallocated volume plane by plane (in parallel), and `SeriesMeta` has `size_z` and `physical_size.z_nm`. Pyramids are xy only (fine for 3D: downsample xy, keep every plane).

Reference data (licences unchecked - local only in `tests/3D/` (gitignored), never commit/ship without checking; only results compared, no code read):
- Viewer/reader: Fiji samples (File > Open Samples) - Fly Brain (RGB volume, 57 planes, 256 × 256, xy 2.68 µm, z 3.95 µm: anisotropy ~1.5; downloaded as `tests/3D/flybrain.tif` from imagej.net/images/flybrain.zip), T1 Head / MRI Stack (16-bit gray), Confocal Series (2 channels × z), Mitosis 5D / Organ of Corti 4D (z with t and c).
- 3D blur / anisotropy: BBBC034 (3D hiPSC monolayer, membrane/mitochondria/DNA, xy 0.065 µm, z 0.29 µm) and the CellProfiler tutorial "3D segmentation of cell monolayer" (github.com/CellProfiler/tutorials, 3d_monolayer) - compare results step by step. Numeric reference: scipy.ndimage.gaussian_filter with an anisotropic sigma.
- 3D segmentation with ground truth: BBBC024 (synthetic 3D HL60 nuclei, 0/25/50/75 % clustering, high/low SNR, 20 nuclei per image, 16-bit label masks); BBBC035 (simulated HL60, Cell Tracking Challenge) is CC-BY - the candidate for committed test data.
- Small unit-test volumes: scikit-image `cells3d` (membrane + nuclei), `kidney` (multi-channel confocal) - licence in the scikit-image data repo README.

### Phase 1 - interfaces ready for 3D (no 3D algorithms yet)
Everything below is shaped as if 3D already worked; 2D fills it with depth 1 / zmin = zmax = z. After this phase, 3D must not need another schema, DB, protocol or enum change.

Done 2026-10-25 (not committed yet; all 3135 tests pass, regression check below):

Results DB (`core/src/storage/duckdb.rs`):
- [x] Schema version: `run.schema_version` = `RESULTS_SCHEMA_VERSION` (1); a file without it reads as 0. The results view refuses a newer file (`reject_newer_results`), adding to an earlier run (`open_existing`) needs the same version.
- [x] `measurements.z_stack` nullable (NULL = the whole volume); the readers take a plane's image values with `(z_stack = z OR z_stack IS NULL)`, joins with `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM`.
- [x] `objects` z columns, each after its x/y ones (area / volume and perimeter / surface area side by side; the Arrow batch follows the table order, pinned by a test): `ndim` (DEFAULT 2, after `t_stack`), `bbox_zmin/zmax_px/_nm`, `centroid_z_px/_nm`, `bbox_center_z_px/_nm`, `volume_px`, `volume_nm3`, `surface_area_nm2`, `touches_z_edge`. A plane's object: zmin = zmax = its plane (NULL for the unknown plane -1), volume NULL. A volume's object: `z_stack` NULL, extent / centroid (`sum_z`) / voxels written, the planar shape columns (area to min_feret) NULL; surface area and `touches_z_edge` NULL until the 3D geometry exists. An unknown z spacing: `pixel_size_z_nm` and the z nm values NULL (was a number).
- [x] Semantics documented in the DDL comments (z_stack, mask plane by plane, touches_edge = xy, planar columns NULL for volumes, 3D shape descriptors as measurement steps).
- [x] Readers: every objects plane filter (list, per image, plate, heatmap, charts, image objects, drawn images) via `ResultsGenerator::object_on_plane_sql` - `z BETWEEN bbox_zmin_px AND bbox_zmax_px`, `z_stack = z` for results without the columns. The tracks query stays on `z_stack` (the tracker is 2D - see 4D below). The results test schema (`test_support.rs`) has the z columns; `ObjectSpec::in_volume`.
- [x] Migration for old results files: not needed - the results format hasn't shipped (before the first release).

Results API / export (`app/src/api/results/column.rs`):
- [x] `Column::VolumePx` / `VolumeNm` / `SurfaceAreaNm` (keys `volume_px`, `volume_nm3`, `surface_area_nm2`), offered in the picker's Geometry group only for results with a volume's object. No columns for z centroid / z box / ndim: x/y centroid and box aren't columns either - add the three axes together if wanted (additive).

Project schema (folded into the unreleased v3 → v4):
- [x] `ZStackHandling::Volume3d`: in the viewer's Z selector as "3D" (shown slice by slice, the Z stepper works); every match handles it; refused by module checks (every command is 2D-only so far), by the job executor and by AI training.
- [x] Annotations (`ObjectMetricSettings`): `dimensionality` and the z moments (`serde(default)`, zeros not written) - bbox / mask stay as they are, a volume's covers its planes z-major.
- [x] Command capability: `#[cmdsmeta(volumes = true)]` -> generated `PipelineCommand::supports_volumes()` (all false today).
- [x] No z settings per command (decided 2026-10-25): a command's extent in z comes from its xy value and the project's (or image's) x/y/z pixel sizes - z = xy value × xy pixel size / z spacing. A per-command override (sigma_z, …) only if users need a different reach in z; it would be additive (`optional` field), nothing to migrate.
- [ ] A 3D run with an unknown z spacing (`PixelSizeSettings.z` None, nothing in the image) is refused with "set the z spacing" (project / image settings) - with the first 3D command.
- [x] Unknown z spacing: `PixelSizeSettings.z: Option<f32>` (None = unknown; old files with a number load unchanged). Core keeps the reader's 0 = unknown, bridged both ways; the UI shows unknown as 0.
- [x] Size filters: `min_area`/`max_area`/`min_intersection_area` (Classify Objects), `min_overlap_area` (Object Math), `min_coloc_area` (Colocalize Objects) -> `…_size`, renamed in v3 → v4 (commands and module bindings); descriptions "area in 2D, volume in 3D". `min_size` (Connected Components) / `min_object_size` were already neutral. Open: a project already saved as v4 (your own test projects) with one of these steps doesn't load ("missing field `minSize`") - the v3 -> v4 step doesn't run on it. Decide: rename the keys in those files by hand, or a v4 -> v5 step.
- [x] Size filters, switching (2026-10-25): switching the Z selector between 2D and 3D shows "Check the size settings" with the size settings changed from their default (`module_insights::size_settings_set`). Still open: the UI showing µm² / µm³ by the image.
- [x] Module files (`.evamodule`): not migrated by design ("updated by hand"); none in the repo.
- [x] **Release blocker** (found 2026-10-25): the templates (repo evanalyzer-templates) still have the old size keys - 5 of 7 don't load ("missing field `minIntersectionSize`"): cell_area_brightfield, cellpose_sam, ev_detection, ev_in_cell_detection, nuclei_detection. Rename `minArea`/`maxArea`/`minIntersectionArea`/`minOverlapArea`/`minColocArea` -> `minSize`/`maxSize`/`minIntersectionSize`/`minOverlapSize`/`minColocSize`, and in the parameter bindings `min_area`/`max_area`/`min_intersection_area` -> `min_size`/`max_size`/`min_intersection_size`; then CI's `check_templates` passes again. - done: the template repo was fixed 2026-10-25 ("chore: Fixed templates"), `check_templates` passes (checked 2026-10-26)

Internal types (`core`):
- [x] `ImageDimensionality { Planar, Volume { depth } }` (cfg `types/image.rs`, `depth()`, `ndim()`); `ManagedImage.dimensionality` + `ImageContainer::dimensionality()`, `ManagedImage::plane_size()` / `plane_slice(z)`. Every construction says it (`Planar`), copies and scratch pads inherit it.
- [x] `ImagePlane`: unchanged - of a volume its first plane (`z` .. `z + depth - 1`), documented.
- [x] `Object` / `ObjectInit`: `dimensionality`, `sum_z`, `sum_z2`, `sum_xz`, `sum_yz` (z counted from `plane.z`, 0 for a plane). Still 2D-only inside (internal, changes with the 3D geometry): `finalize_geometry`, perimeter / ellipse, `TransformedGeometry`, `StoredObject` (z_stack of a volume reads as 0).
- [x] Image reader: `read_image_tile_volume` (planes decoded in parallel straight into one z-major buffer via scopeio `read_region_into`); tests against plane-by-plane reads. The z spacing fallback is the project's `PixelSizeSettings.z` (job generator).
- [x] scopeio read an RGB ImageJ stack's planes as time points (alpha.2) - fixed in 0.0.0-alpha.3: Fiji's Fly Brain reads as one RGB volume of 57 planes (`the_fly_brain_sample_reads_as_an_rgb_volume`, ignored - needs `tests/3D/flybrain.tif`; ~50 ms).
- [x] Remote protocol 31 -> 32: `RawImageInfo.dimensionality` (checked: the height must be whole planes), the new `Column` variants; objects travel with their settings (dimensionality included).

Check at the end of phase 1:
- [x] 2D regression (2026-10-25, BENCHMARK.md "3D-ready results"): `bench_pipeline` 50 × 2048² base vs new - all 60 earlier `objects` columns, `measurements` and `images` identical (ids aside, class lists sorted); only `pixel_size_z_nm` NULL on purpose. Time / RAM within the spread, file +~10 bytes per object.

### Phase 2 - viewer shows volumes (CPU compositor, no GPU pipeline)
- [x] Step 1 - slices (2026-10-25): a volume's object is drawn as its mask's part on the plane shown (`ObjectExt::mask_on_plane` - a sub-slice of the z-major mask, no copy; a plane's object stays on every plane as before), picked on it (`is_part_of_on_plane`), listed only on its planes; a plane change redraws the objects / the list only when there is a volume's object. Results' split view: `image_objects` cuts a volume's object's mask at the plane (decoded, cut, encoded again - only for volumes). `ProjectExt::get_shown_z_plane` (None for a projection: a volume's objects are not drawn then).
- [x] Step 2 - orthogonal views (2026-10-25, `editor/viewport_ortho.rs`): toggle in the Z bar (cube icon); XZ below and YZ right of the image, in bands beside the floating panels (the image's area gives way only while on; off, the layout is exactly as before). No volume cache: only the crosshair's row / column of the visible part is read, at the level shown, every plane in parallel through `ImageSource::read_tile` (local and remote), the last strip per axis kept compact (u8/u16). Own worker (`OrthoWorker`, idle while off), triggered after each sharp render and by the crosshair; a colour / range change only renders again. z drawn true to scale (z spacing / xy pixel size × zoom; unknown z spacing: square, with a note); a stack thicker than the band follows the current plane. Click (no drag) in XY: crosshair; click / drag in XZ / YZ: crosshair and plane. Fly Brain (57 planes, 256²): 7-8 ms per crosshair move (both views read), 0.2-1 ms re-render only, 66 KB kept.
- [x] Step 3 - rotatable 3D (2026-10-25, `editor/viewport_volume.rs`): "3D view" (cube icon) in the Z bar covers the viewport with a maximum intensity projection, turned by dragging, zoomed with the wheel, reset by double click; the channels' colours / display ranges as in the image. The volume read once per image / series / time point, reduced to ≤ 256³ (pyramid level, every n-th pixel / plane), planes compact one by one (never f32 as a whole), freed when off. Own worker (`VolumeWorker`). 256³: 11 / 36 ms while turning (22 / 2 threads), 53 / 244 ms at rest; Fly Brain 3.5-4.5 ms / 24 ms, 11.2 MB kept (BENCHMARK.md). Later, if wanted: alpha blending (surfaces in front), depth cueing / a lower threshold (ImageJ's 3D Project options), an "Animate" around one axis or a video export, trilinear sampling.
- [x] A new pixel size (z spacing) renders the orthogonal and 3D views again right away (2026-10-25; was: only after switching the view).
- [ ] Results views: the split view draws a 3D object as its cut at the current z (done 2026-10-25, see step 1); lists / charts filter planes by the z extent (phase 1). Open: `drawn_images.rs` (exported overlay images) draws boxes per plane only - per plane cut or a maximum projection for volumes.
- [ ] Tiling and RAM: 3D tiles are xy tiles over the full z range; "Preview tiles: Auto" (from the RAM) counts the depth; 3D numbers in the min-RAM estimate.
- [ ] Remote mode: volumes sent plane by plane / streamed (`wire/pixels.rs`), not as one message.

### Phase 3 - first 3D algorithm (3D blur)
Decided 2026-10-25: the existing commands are extended to volumes (as scipy.ndimage / scikit-image / ITK / CellProfiler 3+ - one command, its input's dimensionality picks the path); an own command only where the operation itself differs (e.g. something meaningful only in 3D). Slice by slice stays the project's `ZStackHandling::AllStacks`, no per-command switch.
- [x] Each command declares what it supports (2D only / 2D + 3D); module checks reject a volume into a 2D-only command with a clear message - never a silent per-plane fallback. - done: `PipelineCommand::supports_volumes`, the checks refuse a 2D-only command on volumes (checked 2026-10-26)
- [ ] Per command with its 3D path, decide what "whole image" means - e.g. automatic thresholds (Otsu, …) from the histogram of the whole volume (as CellProfiler), not per plane.
- [ ] Gaussian blur in 3D: the existing xy pass per plane + a 1D pass along z (separable); sigma in z = sigma × xy pixel size / z spacing (the project's pixel sizes, no setting of its own).
- [ ] Tests against a reference 3D Gaussian (anisotropic and isotropic), benchmark speed + RAM (BENCHMARK.md).

### Later (after phase 3)
- 3D object extraction: connected components (6/18/26), stitching across tiles in 3D.
- Tile merge (`tile_merge.rs`) for objects cut at tile borders in 3D.
- 4D (volumes over time): no schema change (`t_stack`, `track_id`, `frame_interval_s` exist) - each t is analysed as one volume, as today per plane. Only the tracker (`dataset/tracking/simple_tracker.rs`) changes: it groups by `(image, z_stack)` - for 3D objects (z_stack NULL) by image only; distance on the 3D centroid with z scaled by the anisotropy, a 3D grid; `max_distance` in px = xy px (z converted), in nm unambiguous. RAM: one volume per t in `pipeline/image_cache.rs`. (Check: its doc says "centre of the bounding box", the code uses `centroid_px` - the centre of mass since 2026-10-23.)
- AI training (pixel / object classifier, `ai_learning_features.rs`): per plane or own 3D features - decide.
- Docs: parameter descriptions "pixels" -> "pixels/voxels", a 3D section in the user docs.
- 3D geometry: voxel count, surface area (voxel faces or marching cubes), ellipsoid, 3D feret, 3D convex hull (expensive, optional) - all with anisotropy.
- 3D filters / morphology / watershed; Relate, Coloc, distances, tracking with 3D masks and centroids.
- 3D AI models (Cellpose 3D, StarDist 3D) - a track of its own.
- Annotations stay per plane; a 3D object is edited as its cut on the slice.


## Usability

- [x] Allow to open evanalyzer modules using the open dialog

---

# TODO: AI-assisted pipeline building

Goal: users describe what they want in plain language ("detect nuclei in channel 0"),
an AI model builds a pipeline, EVAnalyzer runs it locally, and the user verifies and
fine-tunes the result in the normal pipeline editor.

Guiding principles:
- Image pixels stay local by default. Only prompts, pipeline settings and numbers leave the machine.
- The analysis stays deterministic and reproducible. The AI only builds and edits pipelines.
- The model is replaceable (cloud API today, local or on-premise model later).
- Every AI change is a normal, undoable edit.

---

## Phase 1: Foundations (useful even without AI)

- [x] Fix the license inconsistency: README/LICENSE say AGPL-3.0 + commercial,
      `crates/cfg/examples/check_templates.rs` says PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0. - done: README and LICENSE say PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 (checked 2026-10-26)
- [ ] Move overlay rendering (`crates/gui/src/editor/results_overlay.rs`,
      `object_render.rs`) into core so it can run without the GUI.
- [ ] CLI: `evanalyzer render-overlay <project> --image <name> --out overlay.png`.
- [ ] CLI: `evanalyzer preview <project> --image <name> --json`
      (run the pipeline on one image, print summary stats).
- [ ] CLI: `evanalyzer template validate <file.evatemplate>` using the real serde types
      (reuse the logic from `check_templates`).
- [ ] Keep `docs/project_template.schema.json` generated in CI so it never drifts from the code.
- [ ] Add documentation per pipeline command aimed at both humans and models:
      purpose, typical order, parameter ranges, common mistakes
      (e.g. "raise watershed tolerance if objects are over-split").

## Phase 2: Shared tool layer

One Rust module, used by both the chat panel and the MCP server.

- [ ] `get_schema`: the pipeline/template schema (or a compact version of it).
- [ ] `get_project_info`: channels, image count, image size, pixel size, z/t dimensions.
      No file names or paths by default.
- [ ] `get_pipeline` / `set_pipeline`: read and replace the steps of one pipeline.
- [ ] `validate_pipeline`: return precise, human-readable errors.
- [ ] `run_preview`: run on one image, return summary statistics only:
  - [ ] object count per class
  - [ ] size histogram (min, median, max, percentiles)
  - [ ] intensity summary per channel
  - [ ] objects touching the border
  - [ ] objects removed per filter/classify step
- [ ] `render_overlay`: return a PNG. Disabled for cloud models unless the user enables it.
- [ ] Log every tool call with its input and result (for debugging and transparency).

## Phase 3: Chat panel in the GUI (Option A)

- [ ] Chat panel next to the pipeline editor.
- [ ] Model backend abstraction: Claude API first, later local/on-premise models.
- [ ] Settings: API key (stored in the OS keychain), model choice, endpoint URL.
- [ ] Agent loop: send prompt + tool definitions, execute tool calls locally, repeat
      until the model answers, with a maximum number of steps.
- [ ] Pipeline editor updates live when `set_pipeline` is called.
- [ ] Undo/redo for every AI change. Show a diff of what changed.
- [ ] Clear indicator of what is sent to the cloud (prompt, settings, statistics, images yes/no).
- [ ] "Share overlay with AI" toggle, off by default, per message or per session.

## Phase 4: Structured user feedback

Lets the user act as the AI's eyes without sending pixels.

- [ ] Click objects in the overlay and mark them: "should be split", "should be merged",
      "missed object here", "not an object".
- [ ] Send feedback as structured data: object IDs, area, centroid, intensity, class.
- [ ] Quick buttons for common problems: over-segmentation, under-segmentation,
      background detected, dim objects missed.

## Phase 5: MCP server (Option B)

- [ ] `evanalyzer mcp` subcommand: local MCP server over stdio, wrapping the tool layer.
- [ ] Connect to the running GUI so changes appear live (or work on a project file headless).
- [ ] Optional: expose the same tools over HTTP from `crates/server`,
      using its existing TLS, users and sessions.
- [ ] Setup docs for Claude Desktop / Claude Code.

## Phase 6: Trust and validation

- [ ] Reference image set with ground truth (nuclei, cells, spots, 2D and 3D).
- [ ] Benchmark: given a prompt, does the AI-built pipeline reach a target accuracy?
- [ ] Run the benchmark in CI for template and tool changes.
- [ ] Save prompt + final pipeline + model name in the project, so results stay traceable.

